==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|leet}}

===Etymology===
Probably from {{inh|en|enm|lete|t=a meeting or intersection of roads; junction; crossroads; conduit}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ġelǣte|t=a going out, ending, meeting}}, as in {{cog|ang|wæterġelǣt|t=watercourse, aquaduct}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*lētą}}, {{m|gem-pro|*galētą|t=a letting, a letting out}}. Cognate with {{cog|goh|gilāz|t=outlet, exit, end, road junction}}, {{cog|de|Gelass|Gelaß|t=back room, recess, private chamber}}. Related to {{cog|en|let}}.

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun}}

# An [[artificial]] [[watercourse]], [[canal]]{{,}} or [[aqueduct]], but especially a [[millrace]].

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|leatwright}}

